# Abstract

This RFC defines the Collective Intelligence and Shared Memory architecture of Functor.

Functor views intelligence not as a property of individual participants, but as an emergent property of interactions between participants operating within a shared semantic environment.

The system enables:

- Persistent understanding
- Shared knowledge
- Collective memory
- Multi-participant reasoning
- Long-term learning
- Organizational cognition

The objective is not merely to assist intelligence.

The objective is to cultivate intelligence that can be accumulated, shared, refined, and preserved across time.

---

# Motivation

Human cognition is limited.

Agent cognition is limited.

Organizations are limited.

The limitation is not necessarily intelligence itself.

The limitation is continuity.

Understanding is frequently lost:

```text
Conversation Ends

Project Ends

Employee Leaves

Agent Changes

System Migrates
```

Yet the understanding itself remains valuable.

Functor seeks to preserve understanding independently from individual participants.

---

# Core Principle

Understanding should outlive its creators.

---

# Collective Intelligence

Functor adopts the following definition:

```text
Collective Intelligence

The ability of a system of participants
to create understanding greater than any
individual participant could produce alone.
```

Participants may include:

```text
Humans

Agents

Teams

Organizations

Knowledge Assets

Workflows
```

---

# Shared Memory

Shared Memory is the persistent semantic memory of the collective.

Unlike traditional storage systems, Shared Memory stores:

```text
Meaning

Relationships

Intent

Knowledge

Decisions

Understanding
```

rather than merely data.

---

# Memory Hierarchy

Functor defines multiple memory layers.

```text
Memory

├── Session Memory
├── Workflow Memory
├── Team Memory
├── Organizational Memory
└── Collective Memory
```

---

# Session Memory

Short-lived context.

Examples:

```text
Current Discussion

Current Goal

Current Investigation
```

May expire naturally.

---

# Workflow Memory

Memory associated with a particular workflow.

Examples:

```text
Research Notes

Forecast Results

Design Decisions
```

Persists beyond individual sessions.

---

# Team Memory

Knowledge shared by a collaborating team.

Examples:

```text
Processes

Practices

Domain Expertise
```

---

# Organizational Memory

Knowledge preserved at organization scale.

Examples:

```text
Methods

Lessons Learned

Patterns

Institutional Knowledge
```

---

# Collective Memory

The highest memory layer.

Collective Memory represents understanding shared across all participants and assets.

It becomes the semantic heritage of the workspace.

---

# Cognitive Persistence

Functor introduces Cognitive Persistence.

Definition:

```text
The ability of understanding
to survive the participants
that created it.
```

---

# Example

Human:

```text
Creates Insight
```

Agent:

```text
Refines Insight
```

Team:

```text
Applies Insight
```

Years later:

```text
Insight still exists.
```

This is Cognitive Persistence.

---

